ion-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Topham</surname><given-names>M.K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bunting</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zimmerman</surname><given-names>G.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>McIntyre</surname><given-names>T.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Blackshear</surname><given-names>P.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Prescott</surname><given-names>S.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Protein kinase C regulates the nuclear localization of diacylglycerol kinase zeta</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>394</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>697</fpage><lpage>700</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9716136</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="VanBlitterswijkandHoussa1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Van Blitterswijk</surname><given-names>W.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Houssa</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Diacylglycerol kinases in signal transduction</article-title><source>Chem. Phys. Lipids.</source><volume>98</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>95</fpage><lpage>108</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10358932</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yamadaetal1989"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yamada</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sakane</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kanoh</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Immunoquantitation of 80 kDa diacylglycerol kinase in pig and human lymphocytes and several other cells</article-title><source>FEBS Lett</source><volume>244</volume><year>1989</year><fpage>402</fpage><lpage>406</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2537763</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> BAPTA, 1,2-bis(aminophenoxy)ethane-<italic>N</italic>,<italic>N</italic>,<italic>N</italic>&#x02032;,<italic>N</italic>&#x02032;-tetraacetic acid&#x0003b; DGK, DAG kinase&#x0003b; DiC8, 1,2-dioctanoyl <italic>sn</italic>-glycerol&#x0003b; GFP, green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; IL, interleukin&#x0003b; PA, phosphatidic acid&#x0003b; PBut, phosphatidylbutanol&#x0003b; PDBu, 1,2-dioleoylglycerol, phorbol-12,13-dibutyrate&#x0003b; PE, phycoerythrin&#x0003b; PI4,5P<sub>2</sub>, phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate&#x0003b; PLD, phospholipase D&#x0003b; SAX-HPLC, strong ion exchange high performance liquid chromatography&#x0003b; TCR, T cell receptor.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Carbachol induces DGK activation in J-HM1-2.2 cells. (a) Generation of PA after carbachol addition. Cells were metabolically radiolabeled with [<sup>32</sup>P]orthophosphate and stimulated with vehicle or carbachol (50 nM). At the times indicated, cells were collected, total cellular lipids were extracted, and the PA content was determined by SAX-HPLC analysis of deacylated samples (see Materials and Methods). The results are shown of a single experiment representative of three independent experiments. (b) Effect of DGK inhibition on PA generation. Cells were metabolically radiolabeled with [<sup>32</sup>P]orthophosphate and stimulated as indicated. After 2 min, total lipids were extracted, and the PA generated was determined by TLC and quantified as described in Materials and Methods. The top of the figure represents a typical experiment, showing the autoradiogram of the TLC plate corresponding to the area of PA standard migration. The quantification of the PA generated in three independent experiments is shown. (c) Effect of DGK inhibition on DAG generation. Cells were stimulated as indicated: control (C), carbachol (CCh), and R59949 (R59). After 2 min, total lipids were extracted and the level of DAG was quantified as described in Materials and Methods. The quantification of the DAG measured in three independent experiments is shown. (d&#x02013;f) Effect of DGK inhibition on CD69 expression. J-HM1-2.2 cells were stimulated with vehicle (d), carbachol (e), or PDBu + ionomycin (f)&#x0003b; after 6 h, cells were collected and CD69 expression was determined. Filled areas represent CD69 expression by cells after treatment, and empty areas represent cells pretreated with the DGK inhibitor R59949 (10 &#x003bc;M, 10 min) before further stimulation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f1"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Carbachol-mediated plasma membrane translocation of GFP-tagged DGK&#x003b1;. (a) The drawing shows the GFP-DGK&#x003b1; construct used in this study&#x0003b; the conserved cluster in the catalytic domain in which Gly<sup>432</sup> was mutated is marked as a yellow dot. The conserved motifs of the enzyme are indicated. (b) Analysis of carbachol-mediated plasma membrane translocation of GFP-tagged DGK&#x003b1;. J-HM1-2.2 cells were transfected with either GFP-DGK&#x003b1; (top and middle) or a catalytically inactive form of the enzyme (GFP-DGK&#x003b1; kD). After 48 h, cells were treated with vehicle or carbachol for the times indicated. In the middle panel, cells were pretreated with R59949 (10 &#x003bc;M, 10 min) before carbachol stimulation. Cells were fixed and GFP fluorescence was visualized by confocal microscopy. TOPRO-3 was used to visualize nuclei.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f2a"/><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f2b"/></fig><fig id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Constitutive membrane localization of GFP&#x02013;&#x00394;(1-192)DGK&#x003b1; correlates with reduced cell surface CD69 expression. (a) J-HM1-2.2 cells were transfected with plasmids encoding GFP, GFP-DGK&#x003b1;, or GFP&#x02013;&#x00394;(1-192)DGK&#x003b1; plasmids. At 48 h after transfection, cells were left untreated or stimulated with carbachol. CD69 expression was determined by flow cytometry 6 h after carbachol addition to the cells. Histograms show GFP fluorescence (y-axis) plotted versus CD69 expression of control (left) and carbachol-treated (right) cells. (b) Analysis of CD69 expression in GFP-negative versus GFP-positive cells. The CD69 level was determined in the different cell populations after transfection by gating for GFP-negative and GFP-positive cells. CD69 expression in untransfected cells is represented by empty areas and in GFP-positive cells by filled areas. The results shown are from a single experiment representative of three performed with similar results.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f5a"/><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f5b"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Carbachol-mediated plasma membrane translocation of endogenous DGK&#x003b1;. J-HM1-2.2 cells were treated with vehicle or carbachol for 2 min as in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">Fig. 2</xref>. In the bottom panel, cells were pretreated with R59949 (10 &#x003bc;M, 10 min) before carbachol stimulation. Cells were fixed and fluorescence was visualized by confocal microscopy. Nuclei were visualized by staining with Sylver green.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f3"/></fig><fig id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Deletion of the NH<sub>2</sub>-terminal domain of DGK&#x003b1; increases its enzymatic activity and allows constitutive localization in the plasma membrane. (a) COS-7 cells were transfected with vectors encoding GFP, GFP-DGK&#x003b1;, or GFP&#x02013;&#x00394;(1-192)DGK&#x003b1;. At 48 h after transfection, expressed proteins were analyzed by Western blotting with an anti-GFP antibody. (b) Deletion of the EF hand motif induces increased enzymatic activity. DGK activity of the expressed proteins was measured in total lysates of COS-7 cells 48 h after transfection. Radiolabeled PA generated during the reaction was analyzed by TLC and quantified as described in Materials and Methods. The top panel shows an autoradiogram of the TLC plate, and quantification of radioactivity incorporated into PA is shown (bottom). Three independent experiments were performed with similar results. (c) Subcellular distribution of wild-type GFP-DGK&#x003b1;. J-HM1-2.2 (top) and Ba/F3 (bottom) cells were transfected with the GFP-DGK&#x003b1; plasmid. (d) Deletion of the EF hand motif induces constitutive localization to the plasma membrane. J-HM1-2.2 (top) and Ba/F3 (bottom) cells were transfected with the GFP&#x02013;&#x00394;(1-192)DGK&#x003b1; plasmid. GFP fluorescence was determined by confocal microscopy of cells 48 h after transfection. Nuclei were visualized by staining with TOPRO-3.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f4a"/><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f4b"/><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f4cd"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Membrane localization of DGK&#x003b1; correlates with reduced cell surface CD69 expression, whereas lack of enzyme activity enhances CD69 expression. (a) J-HM1-2.2 cells were transfected with plasmids encoding GFP, GFP-DGK&#x003b1;, GFP-DGK&#x003b1;&#x02013;Kd, GFP&#x02013;&#x00394;(1-192)DGK&#x003b1;, or Myr-GFP-DGK&#x003b1; plasmids. At 48 h after transfection, cells were left untreated or stimulated with carbachol. CD69 expression was determined by flow cytometry 6 h after carbachol addition to the cells. Histograms show carbachol-induced CD69 expression in GFP negative (gray bars) and GFP positive (black bars) after transfection of the different constructs. Results shown are representative of a single experiment of two performed with similar results. (b) The cartoon shows the different GFP-DGK&#x003b1; constructs used in these experiments.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Effect of TCR/CD28 cross-linking on the subcellular redistribution of GFP-tagged DGK&#x003b1;. J-HM1-2.2 cells were transfected with GFP-DGK&#x003b1; and after 48 h, were seeded onto slides precoated with anti-CD3 and anti-CD28 antibodies and centrifuged for 20 s at 500 rpm. After incubation for the times indicated, cells were fixed and GFP fluorescence was visualized by confocal microscopy. In the bottom panel, cells were pretreated with R59949 for 10 min before layering onto the slides. TOPRO-3 was used to visualize nuclei.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Pharmacological inhibition of calcium generation and tyrosine kinase activation prevents carbachol-induced translocation of DGK&#x003b1;. Cells were carbachol-stimulated for 2 min after pretreatments with vehicle (a), PLC inhibitor U73122 (10 &#x003bc;M) (b), BAPTA (50 mM) (c), and herbimycin (1 &#x003bc;g/ml) (d). TOPRO-3 was used to visualize nuclei.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f9"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Plasma membrane translocation of GFP-tagged DGK&#x003b1; requires tyrosine kinase activation and intracellular calcium increases. J-HM1-2.2 cells were transfected with GFP-DGK&#x003b1;. After 48 h, the subcellular distribution of the enzyme was determined by confocal microscopy analysis of the cells after 2 min of the following treatments: (a) DiC8 (100 &#x003bc;g/ml)&#x0003b; (b) ionomycin (1 &#x003bc;M) + calcium (2 mM) in the extracellular buffer&#x0003b; (c) DiC8 + ionomycin/calcium&#x0003b; (d) H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (2 mM) + sodium orthovanadate (1 mM)&#x0003b; (e) H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, sodium orthovanadate, and DiC8&#x0003b; and (f) H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, sodium orthovanadate, and ionomycin/calcium.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>A model for receptor regulation of DAG generation and consumption. After receptor stimulation, DAG is generated through PLC activation. Receptor-dependent signals including activation of tyrosine kinases together with a PLC-dependent intracellular calcium increase are responsible for the translocation of the cytosolic/inactive DGK to the plasma membrane. Generation of PA induces the release of the enzyme from the plasma membrane to a cytosolic localization. Membrane localization of the active enzyme (DGK<sup>*</sup>) regulates the intensity of the DAG-derived responses (such as induction of CD69 in this model) by converting DAG to PA.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0008051.f10"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>